book[9,1]="Trauma Analysis Vol.1"
book[9,2]="Helping someone who has experienced something you haven't and couldn't imagine CORRECTLY, is a very hard thing to accomplish. Depending on the situation the correct answer varies but there are some basic do's and don'ts. Starting with the don'ts, do not bombard them with questions. This is not only an annoying thing to do but is irrational and could end with them sealing up completely. Secondly, do not tell them it's not that bad, it could be worse, or that should just 'forget about it'. First of all, you don't know what they're going through and while it could be true, they likely don't care whether or not it could be worse or how bad it is. The way they feel and react is very different than how another human would and you shouldn't assume anything about their situation. Moving on the do's. Sometimes just having someone around is good enough. Especially when they isolate themselves from others, just being there for them is a good way to improve their state of mind. Another thing you should do is to remind them that you are there and offer help. If someone is starting to say that they are worthless and don't matter, simply saying that you are there to help could mean a lot to them. Also, keep in mind not to force yourself into helping them. They might not want help and may just want you to be there for them if they want it in the future. These are just simple guidelines to follow however, you are no trained psychologist. What you really should do, is try and get them professional help. Someone that knows what they are doing and are able to provide deeper and more useful solutions.|p|pActions that will either have a direct or indirect effect on the person with trauma must be done with their consent in order to not accidentally trigger a traumatic response. For example, a hug may trigger a traumatic response if the person has gone through some form of sexual abuse/harassment in their past. In order to get over this trauma (or at the very least its triggers), the person must slowly overcome these triggers with a person they wholeheartedly trust. If they are willing to be hugged by someone who they trust a lot, over time they will become more willing to hug and be hugged by others. To put it simply, it's a form of exposure therapy."
